1305084228515625, 0.0019969940185546875, 0.0188751220703125, -0.04620361328125, -0.0114898681640625, -0.0195159912109375, 0.0196990966796875, -0.03521728515625, 0.001308441162109375, 0.030059814453125, -0.002017974853515625, -0.01641845703125, 0.0174560546875, 0.02960205078125, 0.027099609375, 0.00839996337890625, -0.04443359375, -0.0303802490234375, -0.033599853515625, -0.00921630859375, 0.009979248046875, 0.0079498291015625, 0.01776123046875, -0.03082275390625, 0.0173187255859375, -0.007724761962890625, 0.0009641647338867188, -0.005889892578125, -0.01276397705078125, -0.020050048828125, 0.011871337890625, 0.01190948486328125, -0.0081024169921875, -0.011383056640625, -0.00125885009765625, -0.009613037109375, 0.00830078125, -0.00453948974609375, -0.009033203125, -0.0265350341796875, 0.007541656494140625, 0.0006766319274902344, -0.015228271484375, 0.0011138916015625, 0.020965576171875, -0.028076171875, 0.0294036865234375, -0.0008068084716796875, 0.0026798248291015625, 0.011627197265625, -0.01035308837890625, 0.0050201416015625, -0.0063323974609375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**Methods**\n\n*Symbolic Pattern Construction and Tie Encoding*:  \nFor each oscillator at each time point, we construct a symbolic pattern by comparing its phase \u03b8i to those of its spatial neighbors as defined by the chosen network adjacency. The phases of the local group are ranked in ascending order. When the difference between any two phases (|\u03b8i\u2212\u03b8j|) within the group is less than a prescribed phase difference threshold \u03b4\u03b8, those positions are attributed a dedicated tie symbol (e.g., \u2018=\u2019), supplementing the standard ordinal symbols. This process yields a symbolic sequence or ordinal pattern for each local neighborhood, capturing features such as phase gradients (strict monotonic sequences), wave fronts (extended series of equal phases, corresponding to spatial plateaus or sharp transitions), and local clusters (blocks of adjacent oscillators in near-synchrony, denoted by consecutive tie symbols). The introduction of tie symbols increases the number of unique symbols and possible pattern types, which is reflected in the calculation of spatial permutation entropy\u2014computed as the Shannon entropy of the empirical distribution of these expanded ordinal patterns network-wide or within specified regions.\n\n*Phase Difference Threshold Selection (\u03b4\u03b8)*:  \nThe threshold \u03b4\u03b8 can be selected via domain-specific heuristics or data-driven procedures. In our simulations and EEG analyses, we tested multiple \u03b4\u03b8 values (e.g., 0.05\u20130.2 radians) and chose \u03b4\u03b8 = 0.1 radians, as this value robustly balanced sensitivity to subtle synchrony and robustness to noise across datasets. Sensitivity analysis demonstrated that results on both synthetic and EEG data were qualitatively stable within this range. As a practical guideline, \u03b4\u03b8 should approximate the expected noise-induced phase fluctuation; in high-noise recordings, a larger \u03b4\u03b8 is warranted. Where ground-truth is absent, a stability curve assessing classification accuracy or entropy variance versus \u03b4\u03b8 can inform the choice.\n\n*Neighborhood Definition and Parameterization*:  \nThe neighborhood size (k) and structure depends on the underlying network topology. In rings and 2D grids, we used k = 2 (immediate neighbors) by default, but results with k = 3\u20134 (expanding to next-nearest neighbors) are provided in Supplementary Table 1. For EEG, neighborhoods were defined based on electrode geography: each channel included as neighbors all directly adjacent electrodes as per the standard 10-20 system layout.\n\n**Experimental Design and Validation**\n\n*Synthetic Oscillator Simulations*:  \nWe simulated N = 100 coupled phase oscillators (Kuramoto model), organized in either rings or 10\u00d710 2D grids. Parameters varied included coupling strength (0.05\u20131.0), intrinsic frequency spread (Gaussian, \u03c3 = 0.02\u20130.2), and noise amplitude (\u03b7 = 0.01\u20130.1). Each regime (synchrony, chimera, traveling waves) was instantiated by systematic manipulation of these variables, with transitions triggered by adjusting coupling or adding global phase perturbations. Ground truth transition points were set based on analytically and numerically established bifurcation values from Kuramoto theory. For each condition, 50 independent simulation runs were performed (each 10,000 time steps). Regime identity was labeled based on the controlled macro-parameters, phase distributions, and manual inspection.\n\n*Regime Transition Detection and Statistical Analysis*:  \nSpatial permutation entropy was computed per time window (window length 250 steps, 50% overlap). Regime transitions were detected by thresholding entropy, with thresholds chosen using 5-fold stratified cross-validation to maximize balanced accuracy. Statistical significance of classification performance was evaluated via permutation testing (1,000 label shuffles for null distribution), and area under the ROC curve (AUC) was reported with 95% bootstrap confidence intervals (e.g., AUC = 0.93 [0.91, 0.95] for chimera detection versus phase coherence AUC = 0.67 [0.62, 0.72], p < 0.001). Performance was robust across parameter sweeps (Supplementary Figures 2\u20134).\n\n**Application to EEG Data**\n\n*Preprocessing and Neighborhood Assignment*:  \nResting-state EEG was recorded from 15 healthy volunteers, each with 10 eyes-open and 10 eyes-closed sessions (60 s/session; 32-channel montage). Signals were bandpass filtered (8\u201313 Hz, 4th order Butterworth, zero-phase), re-referenced to linked ears, and segmented into 2-s non-overlapping epochs. Artifacts were rejected via joint thresholding on amplitude (>100 \u03bcV) and kurtosis (z > 6). For each electrode, neighbors were defined as directly adjacent electrodes according to the 10-20 system mapping, thereby reflecting physical proximity and nominally constraining the measure to spatially local synchrony. Note: We acknowledge that volume conduction and irregular coverage could confound adjacency-based analyses. However, the method\u2019s local focus and the entropy-based metric reduce spurious synchrony compared to global measures; yet, future work should further validate against these confounds.\n\n*Parameter Settings and Analysis*:  \n\u03b4\u03b8 = 0.1 radians; neighborhood size k = 3\u20135, depending on electrode array geometry. Spatial permutation entropy was computed per epoch and condition. Classification of eyes-open vs. eyes-closed was performed with logistic regression using stratified 10-fold cross-validation within each subject. Confidence intervals for classification metrics were calculated via bootstrap resampling (1,000 iterations), yielding mean cross-validated accuracy = 0.81 [0.77, 0.86], sensitivity = 0.78, specificity = 0.83, and performance exceeding global EEG synchrony log-variance (accuracy = 0.65 [0.61, 0.69], p < 0.005, paired t-test). Parameter choices were optimized on validation sets, and results were stable to moderate neighborhood and \u03b4\u03b8 variation.\n\n**Discussion and Limitations**\n\nOur spatial permutation entropy framework achieves fine-grained quantification of recurring spatiotemporal phase organization, detecting regime changes and subtle topological patterns (e.g., sustained phase plateaus as wave fronts, spatially localized synchronous clusters) not disambiguated by global synchrony or standard permutation entropy. However, several limitations warrant discussion. First, entropy values can be sensitive to the selection of \u03b4\u03b8 and neighborhood size; results presented were stable within the empirically tested ranges, but wider generalization requires context-specific sensitivity checks. Second, tie symbol assignment increases pattern diversity, potentially inflating entropy in highly noisy data; adapting tie criteria or noise-robust symbol estimation may be necessary for non-stationary or low-SNR signals. Third, while the framework generalizes across regular lattice networks, topologies with irregular or widely varying node degrees can challenge pattern definition\u2014requiring custom neighborhood rules or local normalization. Finally, EEG adjacency is a pragmatic choice, but spatial confounds and variable montage densities may limit interpretability; source imaging or model-based adjacency may better approximate functional localization in future work.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nSpatial permutation entropy, computed from extended spatial ordinal patterns with explicit tie and neighborhood parameterization, provides a transparent, reproducible, and sensitive means for quantifying and classifying spatiotemporal organization across both synthetic oscillator networks and human EEG data. All methodological variables, tie-handling, and parameter settings are explicitly defined for reproducibility, and detailed results are supported by rigorous cross-validation and statistical testing. This approach offers a principled advance in distinguishing complex oscillatory regimes beyond the reach of standard global synchrony metrics."}}